and  determine the accessibility of each  playing pitch in relation to the  surrounding population identify areas  which require additional investment in  order to meet the needs of the local  community interpret the demand for  playing pictures in the district both  now and in the future draw informative  conclusions on the future of playing  pitches in the district and suggest  recommendations for guiding local  policies such as areas requiring  improvements and proposals for the  provision of new playing pitches in line  with national guidance the council is  currently awaiting feedback from Sport  England before we go out to consultation  on the playing pitch strategy  supplementary planning document once the  consultation starts we will advertise  for comments through local and  electronic media including the council's  website where the draft will be  available to download as well as  contacting those on our mailing list if  you wish to be added to the council's  mailing list please listen to the next  article about have your say postcards  are you on the Planning Policy mailing  list to be kept up to date on the local  development framework if so look out for  one of Rochford district councils  postcards coming through your letterbox  across the Rochford district thousands  of residents have asked to be kept  informed about opportunities to have  their say on planning policy  consultations at the moment just over  half of the eight and a half thousand  individuals and companies who have asked  to be kept updated on opportunities to  submit their views on proposed planning  policies are contacted by email while  others receive a letter we want to make  the best use of our available resources  and be more environmentally friendly so  for future consultations those who  currently receive a letter through the  postal service will now start to receive  a postcard instead the postcard will  always be the same size but the  information on the front will order to  reflect which document we are asking you  to comment on the council is currently  working towards the production of a plan  for the district which will set out what  the area will look like in the future  this development plan is known as the  local development framework or ldf the  LDF is a collection of documents which  together will play an important role in  shaping and managing development in the  district documents for me the LDF  include us call strategy and the  allocations document to name just a few  over the last few years we have been  asking for your opinions on planning  policies of the district in order to  ensure that future development reflects  your views in that time we have had a  fantastic response from you and  thousands of comments and suggestions  submitted submitted on a range of  planning matters from the designation of  local wildlife sites to the location of  housing to the future of London's  Southend Airport if you are not on our  mailing list but would like to be  notified of future opportunities to  participate in the production of the LDF  and have access to the internet simply  visit rochford dot j dì hyphen consult  dotnet forward slash  ldf and click register now alternatively  you can contact the planning policy on 0  1 702 318 191 or email planning policy  at Rochford got you k  nine council car parks awarded part mark  status rotifer district council has once  a dane achieved the safer parking scheme  park marked awards for all nine of its  pay and display car parks what is the  safer parking scheme park mark is a  nationwide initiative of the Association  of Chief Police Officers ACPO aimed at  reducing crime and the fear of crime in  parking facilities the scheme is managed  by the British parking association on  behalf of ACP oh and supported by the  Home Office and scottish executive how  are the awards achieved the awards are  granted to parking areas that have  achieved the requirements of both a risk  assessment conducted by the police and a  review of the management practices of  the operator carried out by the british  parking association what has watched the  done to achieve this watch the District  Council manages its own car parks and  over a number of years has made  improvements the help to deter criminal  activity and anti-social behavior we are  committed to doing everything we can to  prevent crime and reduce the fear of  crime in our car parks areas that have  been reviewed our boundaries and  parameters crime recording and  statistics lighting management practice  parking areas pedestrian access signage  surveillance vehicular access why should  I use a pulp mark car park because the  car park has been assessed by the police  and has measures in place that create a  safer environment for both you and your  vehicle visits ww pockmark co uk for  more information  new statistics show that England's lyssa  problem is getting worse the good news  is a national campaign called love where  you live has been launched by the keep  britain tidy group to try and combat  this the campaign intends to raise  awareness about everyone taking  responsibility to care for and protect  where they live by getting involved with  litter picks or other activities which  may help keep the local environment  clean and tidy there are already several  community groups running their own  litter pigs getting involved can be a  great way of meeting people and doing  your bit to keep the district a  beautiful place to live work and visit  the council is encouraging both  individuals and groups to organize their  own litter picks during the official  love where you live week which takes  place during the week commencing the  19th of September 2211 there will be a  series of events taking place across the  country so why not get together with  friends or family and take part it  doesn't matter how long you spent  picking litter or how much you collect  it all helps to improve our district the  love where you live campaign is already  on Facebook and Twitter so log on to  find out more about what's happening and  join up with others to tackle the low  litter problem we'd love to hear your  ideas for love where you live week  events or if you're planning to organize  a letter pick so get in touch using the  local contact details which are you can  email customer services at Rochford gov  UK or call 0170 2318 one double one and  ask for customer services  Rotorua District Council has joined a  new scheme designed to keep residents  informed about air quality not just in  the district but also across the county  the Essex air quality consortium aims to  provide as much information as possible  to people living in Essex about air  quality in the area where you live the  consortium has its own website WWDC org  UK which contains useful information and  educational resources for adults and  children alike the site can give you up  to date readings of air quality  statistics from across the county and  there's also an action-packed section  for young people with lots of games and  information to enjoy rochford District  Council's portfolio holder for the  environment councillor Mike steptoe said  this is a fantastic and very worthwhile  project which aims to ensure that  residents in rochford and across essex  can have the quality of air to match the  quality of life there is here air  pollution does not respect local  authority boundaries so a partnership  such as this where several experts in  their field regularly meet and work  together and share their findings on the  website is a great idea the consortium  consists of all local authorities Essex  County Council the Environment Agency  London Stansted Airport and the  University of Essex  you
 

